                        %mi 𝒞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
          %p Now we look at two examples in more detail: metric spaces and ordinary categories.
          %h3#metric_spaces Metric spaces
          %p
            We consider first the classical case of
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metric+space"} metric space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/poset"} poset
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ≥
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V = ([0, \infty], \geq)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor product
            given by addition. So, to say
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere+metric+space"} Lawvere metric space
            means that with the set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            there is a distance function
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi d
                    %mi X
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi hom
                    %mi X
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d_X = hom_X: X \times X \to [0, \infty]
          %p such that
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo +
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mo ,
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≥
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ≥
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d(x, y) + d(y, z) \geq d(x, z), \qquad 0 \geq d(x, x)
          %p
            for all
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo ,
                  %mi z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x, y, z
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            is a poset.) A
            = succeed "-enriched" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: X \to Y
            here just means a function from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            such that
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi d
                    %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≥
                  %msub
                    %mi d
                    %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d_X(x, y) \geq d_Y(f(x), f(y))
          %p
            for all
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x, y
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            (again, preservation of composition and of identities is superfluous here), so that
            = succeed "-functors" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/short+maps"} short maps
            between metric spaces (Lipschitz maps with constant at most
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            = succeed "-enriched" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo →
                  %mi g
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \to g: X \to Y
            in this case boils down to an instance of a property: that
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ≥
                  %msub
                    %mi d
                    %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \geq d_Y(f(x), g(x))
          %p
            for all
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo ,
                  %mi g
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f, g
            are valued in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0, \infty]
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≥
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(x) \geq g(x)
            for all
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
          %p
            A point of the Cauchy completion is an
            = succeed "-module" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo :
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p: \mathbf{1} \to X
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p: X^{op} \to [0, \infty]
          %p
            for which there is an
            = succeed "-module" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi q
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q: X \to \mathbf{1}
            on the other side, an enriched functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi q
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q: X \to [0, \infty]
          %p
            that is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+adjoint"} right adjoint
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            in the sense of modules. This means there is a unit of the adjunction in the bicategory of modules:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Id
                  %mo :
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mn 1
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi p
                  %mi X
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi q
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (Id: \mathbf{1} \to \mathbf{1}) \to (\mathbf{1} \overset{p}{\to} X \overset{q}{\to} \mathbf{1})
          %p and a counit:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi q
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mn 1
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi p
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Id
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X \overset{q}{\to} \mathbf{1} \overset{p}{\to} X) \to (Id: X \to X)
          %p
            Recall now that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Id
                    %mi X
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Id_X: X \to X
            in the bicategory of modules is the unit bimodule
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi y
                    %mi X
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi V
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi X
                        %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y_X: X \to V^{X^{op}}
            given by the enriched
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+embedding"} Yoneda embedding
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi hom
                      %mi X
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi d
                      %mi X
                    %mo :
                    %msup
                      %mi X
                      %mi op
                    %mo ×
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} hom_X = d_X: X^{op} \times X \to [0, \infty]
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coend"} coend
            formula for a tensor product. If one now tracks through the definitions, keeping in mind that we are in the very simple case of enrichment in a poset, the unit of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunction"} adjunction
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mi q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p \dashv q
            boils down to having the property
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ≥
                  %msup
                    %mo ∫
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi X
                  %mi q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo +
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %munder
                    %mi inf
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi X
                  %mi q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo +
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \geq \int^{x \in X} q(x) + p(x) = \inf_{x \in X} q(x) + p(x)
          %p and the counit boils down to having the property
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo +
                  %mi q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≥
                  %msub
                    %mi d
                    %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p(x) + q(y) \geq d_X(x, y)
          %p
            To better appreciate what these conditions mean, we point out that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p(x)
            should be thought of as the distance
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi d
                    %mover
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d_{\bar{X}}(x, p)
            between
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            and the “ideal point”
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            in the Cauchy completion
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bar{X}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q(x)
            should be thought of as the companion distance
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi d
                      %mover
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi p
                    %mo ,
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d_{\bar{X}}(p, x)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ε
                  %mo &gt;
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varepsilon \gt 0
            there exists
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in X
            such that
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi d
                    %mover
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi p
                  %mo ,
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo &lt;
                  %mi ε
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mi d
                    %mover
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo &lt;
                  %mi ε
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d_{\bar{X}}(p, x) \lt \varepsilon, \, d_{\bar{X}}(x, p) \lt \varepsilon
          %p
            and the counit condition imposes a necessary triangle inequality constraint on the distance functions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bar{X}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo ,
                  %mi p
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p, p'
            are two points of the Cauchy completion thus defined, then their distance is defined by the usual formula for enriched presheaves:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi p
                  %mo ,
                  %mi p
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi X
                  %msub
                    %mi hom
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mn ∞
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi p
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %munder
                    %mi sup
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi X
                  %mi max
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mi p
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo −
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d(p, p') = \int_{x \in X} \hom_{[0, \infty]}(p(x), p'(x)) = \sup_{x \in X} \max\{0, p'(x) - p(x)\}
          %p
            It should be noted that even under the classical definition (where we impose symmetry
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi d
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi y
                    %mo ,
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d(x, y) = d(y, x)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo &gt;
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d(x, y) \gt 0
            for
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ≠
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \neq y
            = succeed ")," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d(x,y) \lt \infty
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bar{X}
            of the embedding of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            into the already complete space of short maps:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi y
                    %mi X
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn ∞
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi X
                        %mi op
                  %mo :
                  %mi x
                  %mo ↦
                  %msub
                    %mi d
                    %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y_X: X \to [0, \infty]^{X^{op}}: x \mapsto d_X(-, x)
          %p
            The presheaf-hom definition of the distance formula for
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bar{X}
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0, \infty]
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi σ
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi r
                  %mo ,
                  %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi r
                  %mo ,
                  %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo +
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi s
                  %mo ,
                  %mi r
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi max
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mi s
                  %mo −
                  %mi r
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo +
                  %mi max
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mi r
                  %mo −
                  %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi r
                  %mo −
                  %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma d(r, s) = d(r, s) + d(s, r) = \max\{0, s-r\} + \max\{0, r-s\} = |r - s|
          %p or equivalently
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi σ
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi r
                  %mo ,
                  %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi r
                  %mo ,
                  %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo +
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi s
                  %mo ,
                  %mi r
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi max
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi max
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mi s
                  %mo −
                  %mi r
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo ,
                  %mi max
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mi r
                  %mo −
                  %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi r
                  %mo −
                  %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma d(r, s) = d(r, s) + d(s, r) = \max(\max\{0, s-r\}, \max\{0, r-s\}) = |r - s|
          %p then we do retrieve the classical formula
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi p
                  %mo ,
                  %mi p
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi X
                  %mi σ
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi p
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %munder
                    %mi sup
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo −
                  %mi p
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d(p, p') = \int_{x \in X} \sigma d(p(x), p'(x)) = \sup_{x \in X} |p(x) - p'(x)|
          %p
            In other words, the completion
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bar{X}
            of a symmetric metric space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            as a general (Lawvere) metric space is not necessarily the same as its completion
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi σ
                  %mover
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma\bar{X}
            as a symmetric metric space, but
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi σ
                  %mover
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma\bar{X}
            is the symmetrisation of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bar{X}
          %h3#ordinary_enriched_categories
            Ordinary (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            \-enriched) categories
          %p
            The analysis of Cauchy complete Lawvere metric spaces contains some of the seeds of what happens in other
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category"} enriched category
            contexts; the case of ordinary
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+category"} small categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/poset"} poset
            but in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+category"} small category
            and let the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/module"} module
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo :
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p: \mathbf{1} \to C
            be a point of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bar{C}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi q
                  %mo :
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q: C \to \mathbf{1}
            as its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+adjoint"} right adjoint
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bicategory"} bicategory
            of modules. As
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi q
                  %mo :
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p: C^{op} \to Set, \, q: C \to Set
          %p
            and the structure of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunction"} adjunction
            is given by unit and counit maps:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi η
                  %mo :
                  %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mo ∫
                    %mrow
                      %mi c
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi Ob
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ×
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                  %msub
                    %mi ε
                    %mrow
                      %mi c
                      %mo ,
                      %mi d
                  %mo :
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ×
                  %mi q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi c
                  %mo ,
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta: 1 \to \int^{c \in Ob(C)} q(c) \times p(c), \qquad \varepsilon_{c, d}: p(c) \times q(d) \to C(c, d)
          %p
            As we said in the case of metric spaces,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p(c)
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q(c)
            measure “distances” = homs:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≅
                  %msup
                    %mi Set
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi C
                        %mi op
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                  %mi q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≅
                  %msup
                    %mi Set
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi C
                        %mi op
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi p
                  %mo ,
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p(c) \cong Set^{C^{op}}(C(-, c), p), \qquad q(c) \cong Set^{C^{op}}(p, C(-, c))
          %p
            The first
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
            is an instance of the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+lemma"} Yoneda lemma
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q(c)
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bimodule"} bimodule
            composite
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mn 1
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi c
                  %mi C
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi q
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathbf{1} \overset{c}{\to} C \overset{q}{\to} \mathbf{1})
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
            is shorthand for the module
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ,
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mi op
                    %mo →
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(-, c): C^{op} \to Set
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+lemma"} Yoneda lemma
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi q
                  %msub
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi C
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo =
                    %mi def
                  %msup
                    %mo ∫
                    %mrow
                      %mi d
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi C
                  %mi q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ×
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi d
                  %mo ,
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo ≅
                    %mi Yoneda
                  %mi q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q \circ_C C(-, c) \overset{def}{=} \int^{d \in C} q(d) \times C(d, c) \overset{Yoneda}{\cong} q(c).
          %p
            Now using the adjunction
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo ⊣
                    %mi q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p \dashv q
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %mi S
                        %mo →
                        %mi q
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi c
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mrow
                        %mi S
                        %mo →
                        %mi q
                        %msub
                          %mo ∘
                          %mi C
                        %mi C
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo ,
                        %mi c
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %mi p
                        %msub
                          %mo ∘
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mn 1
                        %mi S
                        %mo →
                        %mi C
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo ,
                        %mi c
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mrow
                        %mi p
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ×
                        %mi S
                        %mo →
                        %mi C
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo ,
                        %mi c
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{\frac{S \to q(c)}{S \to q \circ_C C(-, c)}}{\frac{p \circ_{\mathbf{1}} S \to C(-, c)}{p(-) \times S \to C(-, c)}}
          %p
            and maps in the bottom line are in bijection with maps
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi Set
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi C
                          %mi op
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi p
                    %mo ,
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ,
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S \to Set^{C^{op}}(p, C(-, c))
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                      %mo →
                      %mi q
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi c
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                      %mo →
                      %msup
                        %mi Set
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi C
                            %mi op
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi p
                      %mo ,
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo ,
                      %mi c
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{S \to q(c)}{S \to Set^{C^{op}}(p, C(-, c))}
          %p
            and this proves
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi q
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≅
                    %msup
                      %mi Set
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi C
                          %mi op
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi p
                    %mo ,
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ,
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q(c) \cong Set^{C^{op}}(p, C(-, c))
          %p
            With these identifications of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q(c)
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p(c)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mi q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p \dashv q
            takes the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi η
                  %mo :
                  %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi c
                  %msup
                    %mi Set
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi C
                        %mi op
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi p
                  %mo ,
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ×
                  %msup
                    %mi Set
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi C
                        %mi op
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta: 1 \to \int^{c} Set^{C^{op}}(p, C(-, c)) \times Set^{C^{op}}(C(-, c), p)
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coend"} coend
            above is a quotient of
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mi c
                  %msup
                    %mi Set
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi C
                        %mi op
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi p
                  %mo ,
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ×
                  %msup
                    %mi Set
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi C
                        %mi op
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sum_c Set^{C^{op}}(p, C(-, c)) \times Set^{C^{op}}(C(-, c), p)
          %p
            and hence the unit element
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi η
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta
            is represented by a pair of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformation"} transformation
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                  %mo :
                  %mi p
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                  %mi π
                  %mo :
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i: p \to C(-, c), \qquad \pi: C(-, c) \to p
          %p
            for some
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
          %p Given that, it is now not hard – in fact it is fairly tautological – to verify that on the basis of the triangular equation of the adjunction which says
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi p
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo ∘
                      %mi η
                  %mi p
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi q
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi p
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi ε
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mn 1
                    %mi p
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (p \overset{p \circ \eta}{\to} p \circ q \circ p \overset{\varepsilon}{\to} p) = 1_p,
          %p that
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi p
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi i
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi π
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mn 1
                    %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (p \overset{i}{\to} C(-, c) \overset{\pi}{\to} p) = 1_p
          %p
            and so a point
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            in the Cauchy completion
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bar{C}
            must be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/retract"} retract
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representable+functor"} representable
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ,
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(-, c)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi π
                  %mi p
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi i
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(-, c) \overset{\pi}{\to} p \overset{i}{\to} C(-, c)
          %p
            is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/idempotent"} idempotent
            represented by a morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi e
                  %mo :
                  %mi c
                  %mo →
                  %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e: c \to c
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            (by the Yoneda lemma), and this factorization through
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/split+idempotent"} splits
            the idempotent
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mi e
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(-, e)
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Set
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi C
                          %mi op
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set^{C^{op}}
          %p
            Indeed, the claim is that modules
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p: C^{op} \to Set
            in the Cauchy completion are precisely those presheaves on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            which arise as
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/retract"} retract
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representable+functor"} representable
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Set
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi C
                          %mi op
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set^{C^{op}}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/split+idempotent"} idempotent-splitting
            completion of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            (aka the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Karoubi+envelope"} Karoubi envelope
            of
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            = succeed "-enriched" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            %em is
            the idempotent-splitting completion. In particular, representables themselves are points of the Cauchy completion.
          %p
            Notice that in a finitely complete category (such as
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            or a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaf"} presheaf
            category), idempotents
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi e
                  %mo :
                  %mi c
                  %mo →
                  %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e: c \to c
            split automatically: just take the equalizer of the pair
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                  %mover
                    %munder
                      %mo →
                      %mn 1
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mi e
                  %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c \stackrel{\overset{e}{\to}}{\underset{1}{\to}} c
          %p
            For that matter, in any finitely cocomplete category, taking the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coequalizer"} coequalizer
            of the above pair would also split the idempotent. Indeed, we can say that idempotents split in a category iff all equalizers of such pairs exist, iff all coequalizers of such pairs exist.
          %p
            Notice that if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            are categories, then any functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo :
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F: C \to D
            preserves
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/retract"} retract
            %em any
            functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            whatsoever. They are called
            %strong absolute limits
            for that reason. For the same reason, the coequalizers above are
            = succeed ":" do
              %strong absolute colimits
          %p
            Pursuing this a bit further: if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F: C^{op} \to Set
            is any functor, then (because idempotents split in
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mover
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bar{F}: \bar{C}^{op} \to Set
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Set
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi C
                        %mi op
                  %mo ≃
                  %msup
                    %mi Set
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mover
                          %mi C
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                        %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set^{C^{op}} \simeq Set^{\bar{C}^{op}}
          %p
            and we say that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bar{C}
            are
            = succeed "." do
              %strong Morita equivalent
          %h3#of_posets Of posets
          %p
            Every ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/poset"} poset
            clearly is Cauchy complete, since the only idempotents are the
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+morphism"} identity morphism
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} internalization
            of this statement requires some extra assumptions:
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_6 Proposition
            %p
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} Internal
              to any
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+category"} regular category
              every
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/poset"} poset
            %p is Cauchy complete.
          %p
            This appears as (
            %a{:href => "#Rosolini"}> Rosolini, prop. 2.1
            ).
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_7 Proposition
            %p
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} Internal to
              any
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exact+category"} exact category
              the Cauchy completion of any
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/preorder"} preorder
              exists and is its
              = succeed "." do
                %span.newWikiWord
                  poset reflection
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/poset+reflection"} ?
          %p
            This appears as (
            %a{:href => "#Rosolini"}> Rosolini, corollary. 2.3
            ).
          %p
            Moreover, the characterization of Cauchy completion by left adjoint profunctors requires the internal
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_8 Proposition
            %p In a given ambient context, the following are equivalent:
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
                  holds;
              %li
                %p
                  every
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/profunctor"} profunctor
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi F
                        %mo :
                        %mi A
                        %mi ⇸
                        %mi C
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F : A &amp;#8696; C
                  between
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/posets"} posets
                  is an ordinary functor when it has a
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+adjoint"} right adjoint
          %p
            For instance (
            %a{:href => "#BorceuxDejean"}> BorceuxDejean, prop. 5
            ).
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/split+idempotent"} split idempotent
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Karoubi+envelope"} Karoubi envelope
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+cocompletion"}> free cocompletion
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+coproduct+completion"} free coproduct completion
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/idempotent+complete+%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} idempotent complete (infinity,1)-category
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+bicompletion"} free bicompletion
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reflexive+completion"} reflexive completion
          %h2#references References
          %p The notion of Cauchy complete categories was introduced in
          %ul
            %li#Lawvere
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bill+Lawvere"}> Bill Lawvere
                ,
                %em Metric spaces, generalized logic and closed categories
                Rend. Sem. Mat. Fis. Milano, 43:135–166 (1973)
              %p
                Reprints in Theory and Applications of Categories, No. 1 (2002) pp 1-37 (
                %a{:href => "http://www.tac.mta.ca/tac/reprints/articles/1/tr1abs.html"}> tac
                )
          %p Surveys are in
          %ul#CarboniStreet
            %li
              %p#BorceuxDejean
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Francis+Borceux"} Francis Borceux
                and D. Dejean,
                %em Cauchy completion in category theory
                Cahiers Topologie Géom. Différentielle Catégoriques, 27:133–146, (1986) (
                %a{:href => "http://www.numdam.org/item?id=CTGDC_1986__27_2_133_0"}> numdam
                )
            %li
              %p
                A. Carboni and
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ross+Street"} Ross Street
                %em Order ideal in categories
                Pacific J. Math., 124:275–288, 1986.
          %p Further references include for instance
          %ul
            %li#J1989
              %p
                S. R. Johnson,
                %em Small Cauchy completions
                , JPAA
                %strong 62
                (1989) pp.35-45. (
                %a{:href => "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/0022404989900194"}> web
                )
            %li
              %p
                R. Walters,
                %em Sheaves and Cauchy complete categories
                , Cahiers Top. Geom. Diff. Cat. 22 no. 3 (1981) 283-286 (
                %a{:href => "http://www.numdam.org/item?id=CTGDC_1981__22_3_283_0"}> numdam
                )
            %li
              %p
                R. Walters,
                %em Sheaves on sites as Cauchy-complete categories, J. Pure Appl. Algebra 24 (1982) 95-102
            %li#NST2020
              %p
                Branko Nikolić,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ross+Street"} Ross Street
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Giacomo+Tendas"} Giacomo Tendas
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Cauchy completeness for DG-categories
            %li#LT2022
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stephen+Lack"}> Stephen Lack
                ,
                = succeed "," do
